WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Third block.

2
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:09.525
Proteins are built from reusable parts, they exist in more than one state, and then we turn to structure as something to predict.

3
00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:14.400
A domain is a compact, semi-independent unit of structure.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:24.400
It can often fold on its own, and it is reused across many different proteins — which is why classifying domains is more useful than classifying whole proteins.

5
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.250
Sessions three and five met domains from the sequence side, as Pfam families built from profile HMMs.

6
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:40.275
Structural classifications group them by three-dimensional shape instead, and that can reveal relationships sequence misses — as we have just seen.

7
00:00:40.275 --> 00:00:47.250
CATH, from Orengo and colleagues in nineteen ninety-seven, uses four levels, and the name is the levels.

8
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.425
Class: mainly alpha, mainly beta, alpha-beta, or few secondary structures.

9
00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:57.200
Architecture: the overall arrangement of the secondary-structure elements.

10
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.475
Topology: how they are connected, which is what people usually mean by the fold.

11
00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:07.600
And Homologous superfamily: domains with evidence of common ancestry.

12
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:17.425
Beta-globin's domain is one point ten point four nine zero point one zero — Mainly Alpha, then Orthogonal Bundle, then Globin-like, then Globins.

13
00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:24.800
Each number is one level, narrowing from a very broad structural class to a specific family with shared ancestry.

14
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.650
Myoglobin is in the same place.

15
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.825
So is neuroglobin.

16
00:01:29.825 --> 00:01:37.825
And so is the Mycobacterium tuberculosis truncated haemoglobin — the one whose sequence alignment scored below noise.

17
00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:42.325
GFP, meanwhile, is in class two, Mainly Beta, Beta Barrel.

18
00:01:42.925 --> 00:01:47.425
This disagreement is worth dwelling on, because both sides are right.

19
00:01:47.425 --> 00:01:53.675
CATH puts beta-globin and the truncated haemoglobin in the same homologous superfamily.

20
00:01:53.675 --> 00:01:56.625
Pfam, working from sequence, cannot.

21
00:01:56.625 --> 00:02:10.600
It needs a separate family for the truncated haemoglobin — PF01152, "Bacterial-like globin", rather than PF00042, "Globin" — and connects the two only at the higher level of a clan.

22
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.300
Neither classification is wrong.

23
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.400
They are answering with the evidence they have.

24
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:24.475
The sequence similarity genuinely is too weak to support one family; we measured that, and it scored below shuffled sequences.

25
00:02:24.475 --> 00:02:31.000
The structural similarity genuinely is strong enough to support one superfamily; we measured that too.

26
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:41.675
So the two classifications are complementary rather than competing, and if you want to know whether two proteins are related, the answer may depend on which kind of evidence you ask for.

27
00:02:41.675 --> 00:02:51.650
The practical habit: when a sequence-based resource says two proteins are unrelated, that is a statement about sequence evidence, not a verdict on ancestry.

28
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:58.100
CATH is not the only one, and the others differ in philosophy rather than in detail.

29
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:08.025
SCOP, from Murzin and colleagues in nineteen ninety-five, is built on expert manual curation — a human structural biologist deciding where the boundaries are.

30
00:03:08.025 --> 00:03:16.275
ECOD, Cheng and colleagues, two thousand and fourteen, is organised primarily by evolutionary relationship rather than by shape.

31
00:03:16.275 --> 00:03:33.425
And TED, from Lau, Bordin and colleagues in twenty twenty-four, parses domains automatically out of the entire AlphaFold Database — hundreds of millions of predicted structures, far more than anyone could curate by hand, and mostly structures nobody has ever solved experimentally.

32
00:03:33.425 --> 00:03:35.750
They do not always agree.

33
00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:41.675
They disagree about where one domain ends, and about how distant superfamilies should be grouped.

34
00:03:41.675 --> 00:03:49.575
That is not a scandal: domains are a useful abstraction over a continuum, and a continuum does not have natural boundaries.

35
00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:56.500
So knowing that several classifications exist, and why they differ, matters more than picking one and trusting it.

36
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:04.400
Beyond single chains, a complex is held together by interfaces — patches of surface where two chains touch.

37
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.875
Interfaces have measurable properties that distinguish them from the rest of the surface.

38
00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:18.350
They are usually more hydrophobic, because burying oily residues against another protein is energetically much like burying them in a core.

39
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.425
And they are usually more conserved, because they have a job to do.

40
00:04:22.425 --> 00:04:33.325
Both of those are reasons to hope a predictor could find them from sequence and structure, and the fact that they are properties rather than motifs is why that is a learning problem rather than a lookup.

41
00:04:33.325 --> 00:04:40.250
Many proteins also bind ligands — small molecules or ions, such as haemoglobin's four haem groups.

42
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:46.200
Interfaces and ligand-binding sites are where much of a protein's function actually happens.

43
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.750
A fold is a scaffold; the function is usually at a site on it.

44
00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:55.450
Predicting those sites is one of the open problems of Session twelve.

45
00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:05.825
If interfaces are where the function is, the obvious question is whether we can predict them — given two proteins of known structure, work out how they fit together.

46
00:05:05.825 --> 00:05:14.475
That is called docking, and it has its own history running alongside single-chain prediction, along the same three routes you just saw for folds.

47
00:05:14.475 --> 00:05:16.750
Shape and physics first.

48
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:32.075
In nineteen ninety-two Katchalski-Katzir and colleagues put both proteins on a three-dimensional grid and scored shape complementarity for every relative translation at once, using the fast Fourier transform, while sampling rotations exhaustively.

49
00:05:32.075 --> 00:05:39.825
The trick is that the FFT turns "try every offset" into a single calculation rather than millions of separate ones.

50
00:05:39.825 --> 00:05:53.100
That became the standard engine, and the refinements followed: GRAMM used low-resolution grids that tolerate errors in the input structures, and ZDOCK added electrostatics and desolvation to the shape term.

51
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.700
All of those produce thousands of candidate poses.

52
00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:02.850
And that is the real difficulty — not generating an answer, but ranking the ones you already have.

53
00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:08.525
A method that puts the right answer somewhere in its top thousand has not solved the problem.

54
00:06:08.525 --> 00:06:10.750
Second route: data.

55
00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:21.800
HADDOCK, from two thousand and three, drives the search with experimental information — mutations that abolish binding, NMR data — marking which residues are at the interface.

56
00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:27.500
Rather than searching blind, you constrain the search with what the experiments already say.

57
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.825
Third route: templates.

58
00:06:29.825 --> 00:06:39.050
A complex can be modelled on a related complex whose structure is already known, exactly as comparative modelling builds a chain on a related chain.

59
00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:48.725
The claim from twenty twelve is stronger than you might expect: templates "are available to model nearly all complexes of structurally characterized proteins".

60
00:06:48.725 --> 00:06:53.500
The first author of that paper, Petras Kundrotas, teaches on this course.

61
00:06:54.100 --> 00:07:00.125
Measuring how good a predicted complex is needs its own measures, and the reason is specific.

62
00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:04.850
Both chains can be modelled beautifully and still be put together wrongly.

63
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:15.300
A single RMSD over every atom would average the good chains together with the bad placement and report something mediocre, which tells you nothing about which part failed.

64
00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:22.350
So the convention is to superimpose on the larger chain — the receptor — and then measure the error on the smaller one.

65
00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:30.975
That is ligand RMSD, and it isolates exactly the thing in question: given that you got the receptor right, where did you put the other chain?

66
00:07:30.975 --> 00:07:42.375
Interface RMSD measures only over the residues in contact, which asks a slightly different question: is the interface itself right, even if the far ends of the chains have drifted?

67
00:07:42.375 --> 00:07:52.275
A third kind of measure drops distances altogether and counts contacts — what fraction of the real complex's residue-residue contacts does the model reproduce?

68
00:07:52.275 --> 00:07:57.425
That is closer to asking whether the model would make the same biological predictions.

69
00:07:57.425 --> 00:08:04.625
And DockQ, from Basu and Wallner in twenty sixteen, combines these into a single number between zero and one.

70
00:08:04.625 --> 00:08:15.700
That is the figure usually quoted, which is why, when Session twelve talks about how confident AlphaFold is about an interface, the honest measure it gets compared against is DockQ.

71
00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:20.675
Docking has had its own blind assessment since two thousand and one.

72
00:08:20.675 --> 00:08:27.100
CAPRI is docking's CASP, and until twenty eighteen its verdict was strikingly consistent.

73
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:31.950
Targets with a template available were predicted "very good to excellent".

74
00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:43.025
The template route worked, for the same reason comparative modelling works for single chains: if you can find a solved complex of related proteins, you can borrow its arrangement.

75
00:08:43.025 --> 00:08:46.375
Targets without a template were another matter.

76
00:08:46.375 --> 00:08:54.800
Of eleven difficult targets at CASP13 in twenty eighteen, medium or high quality models were submitted for only three.

77
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.875
Three out of eleven.

78
00:08:56.875 --> 00:09:05.200
And notice how closely that mirrors the single-chain story before twenty twenty — with a template, reliable; without one, mostly not.

79
00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.025
Docking had the same shape of problem and the same ceiling.

80
00:09:09.025 --> 00:09:15.800
What changed it is also the same thing that changed single-chain prediction, and that is Session twelve's subject.

81
00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.650
Now the joke pays off.

82
00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.600
Haemoglobin has two classic states.

83
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.600
Deoxy — T, for tense, PDB entry two-H-H-B.

84
00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.775
And oxy — R, for relaxed, one-H-H-O.

85
00:09:28.775 --> 00:09:34.100
We already measured the chains: TM-score nought point nine eight between the two states.

86
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.900
Each chain hardly changes at all.

87
00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.700
So the difference is not in the folds.

88
00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:43.025
It is in how the folded chains are arranged.

89
00:09:43.025 --> 00:09:55.250
The two alpha-beta pairs rotate relative to each other by about thirteen to fifteen degrees — Baldwin and Chothia measured this in nineteen seventy-nine, and the third discussion problem has you measure it yourself.

90
00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:58.025
The notebook measures a consequence.

91
00:09:58.025 --> 00:10:12.200
The distance between the iron atoms of the two beta-chain haems is thirty-nine point five one ångström in the T state and thirty-four point six two in the R state — a contraction of four point eight nine ångström, call it five.

92
00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:15.400
And that quaternary change is the mechanism.

93
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:26.700
It is how binding at one subunit makes binding at the others easier, which is cooperative oxygen binding, which is what lets haemoglobin pick oxygen up in the lungs and let it go in the tissues.

94
00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:32.975
A protein that bound oxygen without cooperativity would be far worse at being haemoglobin.

95
00:10:32.975 --> 00:10:35.875
Give me some oxygen and I'll relax.

96
00:10:35.875 --> 00:10:41.125
That is the T-to-R transition, and it is quaternary structure doing the work.

97
00:10:41.725 --> 00:10:46.925
Which leads to the general lesson, and it is one of the most useful things in this session.

98
00:10:46.925 --> 00:10:53.625
A PDB entry is one snapshot of one state, under one set of conditions, usually in a crystal.

99
00:10:53.625 --> 00:10:56.625
It is not "the" structure of the protein.

100
00:10:56.625 --> 00:11:02.900
Proteins occupy different conformational states depending on ligands, partners and conditions.

101
00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:06.150
Haemoglobin has two and they are both correct.

102
00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:12.125
Ask which one is the real structure of haemoglobin and you have asked a question with no answer.

103
00:11:12.125 --> 00:11:17.100
Here are the two measurements again, because together they make the point precisely.

104
00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:22.425
TM-score of each chain, T against R: nought point nine eight — nothing changed.

105
00:11:22.425 --> 00:11:29.725
Iron-to-iron distance across the beta chains: about five ångström shorter in R — everything changed.

106
00:11:29.725 --> 00:11:36.250
Both true at once, which is only possible because the change is in the arrangement rather than in the folds.

107
00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:44.725
Why this matters for the rest of the course: whenever a predicted model is compared with "the" experimental structure, this caveat applies.

108
00:11:44.725 --> 00:11:47.775
AlphaFold usually predicts one state.

109
00:11:47.775 --> 00:11:53.925
If a protein has two, and the prediction matches one of them, is that a success or a half-failure?

110
00:11:53.925 --> 00:11:59.550
Session twelve returns to this as one of the real limitations of structure prediction.

111
00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:02.725
Now we turn the session around.

112
00:12:02.725 --> 00:12:08.875
Given only the sequence, could a computer predict which residues become helix, strand or coil?

113
00:12:08.875 --> 00:12:15.450
As a supervised learning problem: the input is the sequence, or its profile, one window per residue.

114
00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:20.550
The target is H, E or C — helix, strand or coil — for every residue.

115
00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:27.150
The training labels come from solved structures, assigned by DSSP, exactly as in the notebook.

116
00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:35.050
That is a complete specification of a machine-learning task, and it is worth noticing that we could only write it because of this session.

117
00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:39.900
We needed structures to label from, and a program to assign the labels.

118
00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.275
What information could help?

119
00:12:42.275 --> 00:12:44.150
Three sources.

120
00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:51.625
The residue itself, because some amino acids favour helices, others strands, and proline and glycine break both.

121
00:12:51.625 --> 00:12:55.825
Its neighbours in the sequence, since secondary structure is local.

122
00:12:55.825 --> 00:13:02.775
And — from Session five — how that position varies across homologous sequences, which is to say a profile.

123
00:13:02.775 --> 00:13:13.525
That third one turns out to be the most useful input of the three, which is a nice closing of the loop: the profile methods of Session five become the input features of Session seven.

124
00:13:13.525 --> 00:13:24.575
Sessions seven to eleven build the machinery for learning mappings like this, and the course returns to this specific problem in Sessions seven, nine and ten, and in the capstone project.

125
00:13:25.175 --> 00:13:31.550
Predicting the full three-dimensional structure has been one of biology's hardest problems for decades.

126
00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:45.675
Since nineteen ninety-four the CASP competition — Critical Assessment of Structure Prediction — has tested methods blind, on structures that are solved but not yet published, which is the only honest way to run such a test.

127
00:13:45.675 --> 00:13:50.550
Four classical strategies, each building on something you have already met.

128
00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:59.275
Physics, also called de novo or ab initio modelling, searches for the lowest-energy arrangement of the chain using physical interactions.

129
00:13:59.275 --> 00:14:04.975
Rigorous in principle, and so expensive that it long worked only for small proteins.

130
00:14:04.975 --> 00:14:17.550
Rosetta, from the Baker lab, assembles structures from short fragments of known structures instead of searching from nothing — and it grew into a tool for protein design, which is what the Nobel Prize recognised.

131
00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:26.775
Comparative, or homology, modelling finds a related protein of known structure — Sessions four and five — and builds the model on it as a template.

132
00:14:26.775 --> 00:14:30.975
Reliable when a template exists, useless for a new fold.

133
00:14:30.975 --> 00:14:41.300
And the table from the second block is exactly why it works: related sequences share their structure, so a structure you can find by sequence search is a structure you can borrow.

134
00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:48.350
Contact prediction uses the fact that residues in contact tend to mutate together, which is the next slide.

135
00:14:48.350 --> 00:15:00.200
And deep learning arrived with AlphaFold2 in twenty twenty, combining learned representations, evolutionary information and geometry, and it was the breakthrough at CASP14.

136
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:06.450
Session twelve covers it, once Sessions seven to eleven have built up the neural-network machinery.

137
00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:13.775
The coevolution idea deserves its own slide, because it is the bridge from Session five to AlphaFold.

138
00:15:13.775 --> 00:15:16.100
The reasoning is physical.

139
00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:23.725
Suppose a positively charged residue at one position faces a negatively charged one at another, across a contact.

140
00:15:23.725 --> 00:15:31.775
A mutation that makes the first position negative is destabilising — unless the second position also changes to compensate.

141
00:15:31.775 --> 00:15:37.050
So the two positions are not free to vary independently; they vary together.

142
00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:43.075
Now look across thousands of homologous sequences, which Session five taught you how to collect.

143
00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:47.850
Correlated columns in that alignment point to residues in contact.

144
00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:59.500
The signal is statistical and it is about pairs, which is exactly the information a single sequence cannot carry and a profile — scoring each position independently — cannot represent.

145
00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:03.625
That is the link back to Session five's closing limitation.

146
00:16:03.625 --> 00:16:12.225
A PSSM and a profile HMM both score positions independently, so neither can express a correlation between two columns.

147
00:16:12.225 --> 00:16:16.825
Coevolution analysis is precisely about those correlations.

148
00:16:16.825 --> 00:16:23.875
Fill in enough correlated pairs and you have a contact map, which is the representation from the first block of today.

149
00:16:23.875 --> 00:16:29.100
And a contact map is close enough to a structure that geometry can finish the job.

150
00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:32.850
Session twelve follows that path through to AlphaFold.

151
00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:39.400
One last thing, pointing forward, and it starts from a question this session has already set up.

152
00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:51.575
AF-Cluster: clustering a multiple sequence alignment, rather than using it whole, lets AlphaFold2 predict both folds of KaiB, a protein that genuinely switches between two different structures.

153
00:16:51.575 --> 00:17:01.375
NMR confirmed one of those predictions, and three designed mutations flipped a KaiB variant to the other state — which is a prediction that was tested and held.

154
00:17:01.375 --> 00:17:15.650
At the same time, in-cell cryo-electron tomography combined with cross-linking mass spectrometry showed RNA polymerase and the ribosome coupled together by NusG and NusA, inside living Mycoplasma pneumoniae cells.

155
00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:20.550
Not a purified complex in a crystal: the machine in place, in the cell.

156
00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:32.275
And the twenty twenty-four Nobel Prize in Chemistry went half to David Baker for computational protein design, and half to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for protein structure prediction.

157
00:17:32.275 --> 00:17:36.450
So here is the question to carry into the second half of the course.

158
00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:43.125
Haemoglobin's function is the switch between T and R — not either state, but the transition between them.

159
00:17:43.125 --> 00:17:48.225
We measured both states today, and each chain is almost identical in the two.

160
00:17:48.225 --> 00:17:51.150
What would one predicted structure miss?

161
00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:58.150
If a prediction server gives you one confident model of haemoglobin, it has given you a protein that cannot breathe.

162
00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:02.650
The answer is not that prediction is useless; it plainly is not.

163
00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:12.450
The answer is that "the structure" was always a simplification, and now that we can predict structures easily, the simplification has become the thing worth questioning.

164
00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.750
To put the session in context.

165
00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.300
Today: structures as data.

166
00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:23.850
Compared by alignment, by TM-score, by distance maps, and searched with Foldseek.

167
00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:28.275
Plus the reminder that one experimental structure is one state.

168
00:18:28.275 --> 00:18:33.725
And the course's path through representations, which is the thread to follow from here.

169
00:18:33.725 --> 00:18:36.575
Sequence, in Sessions three and four.

170
00:18:36.575 --> 00:18:40.550
Profile and evolutionary information, Session five.

171
00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:44.475
Learned representations, Sessions seven to eleven.

172
00:18:44.475 --> 00:18:49.675
Local structure, contacts and distances — Sessions seven, ten and twelve.

173
00:18:49.675 --> 00:18:53.775
And finally three-dimensional structure, in Session twelve.

174
00:18:53.775 --> 00:19:02.250
Each step is a change of representation, and at each step more of the representation is learned from data rather than designed by hand.

175
00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:09.550
Foldseek's 3Di alphabet, which you met today, is the first one on that list where a machine chose the representation.

176
00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:11.650
It will not be the last.

177
00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:14.450
That is Session six.

178
00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:37.850
The notebook fetches both haemoglobin structures, reads the chains and residues, computes the secondary structure with PyMOL and compares it with the PDB annotation that Session seven uses, renders the tetramer, draws beta-globin's distance and contact maps, builds the sequence-versus-structure table, and measures the iron-to-iron distance that changes by about five ångström between the states.

179
00:19:37.850 --> 00:20:04.725
The lab runs PyMOL as a Python library inside the notebook, on three structures beyond haemoglobin: an HIV envelope glycoprotein trimer with an antibody for representations, chains and structural alignment; a chitinase with bound metal ions and an inhibitor for ligands and charged residues; and human neuroglobin for the central comparison, run with TM-align — the same protein experimental against AlphaFold prediction, a different globin, and GFP.

180
00:20:04.725 --> 00:20:13.650
Then a search of the whole PDB with neuroglobin through the Foldseek web server's API, and a structural domain lookup in CATH.

181
00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:18.700
Session seven starts building the machinery to predict what we have been looking at.

182
00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:36.325
It returns to the weighted sum a PSSM computes, formalises it as a perceptron whose weights are learned from labelled examples, applies it to secondary structure through Rost and Sander's PHD method, and shows why the helix you saw in PyMOL can be hard to predict from a window of residues.

183
00:20:36.325 --> 00:20:37.950
See you there.

